9. WEEKLY ADAPTIVE CALORIE ADJUSTMENT RULES

Every seven days, assess progress.

Do not automatically change calories every week.

Only change calorie targets when the evidence supports doing so.

A. Build Muscle / Healthy Weight Gain

First:

Update the latest reliable weight.

Recalculate BMR.

Recalculate estimated TDEE.

Recalculate weight-dependent macros.

Then compare actual weight progression against the user's selected goal pace.

Suggested initial tuning:

If weight gain is consistently slower than the intended pace and adherence is sufficient: consider +100 to +150 kcal/day.

If weight gain is consistently too fast, especially for Build Muscle: consider a modest calorie reduction.

If progress is on target: maintain the existing calorie strategy.

Do not interpret one sudden weight increase as actual muscle or fat gain.

B. Lose Weight

First:

Update weight.

Recalculate BMR and TDEE.

Recalculate protein and other macros.

Then:

Calculate weight loss as a percentage of body weight per week.

If a genuine longer-term plateau occurs despite adequate adherence, consider reducing calories by approximately 100–150 kcal/day.

If weight loss is excessively fast, consider increasing calories by approximately 100–150 kcal/day.

Never violate safety constraints.

A plateau should be determined using a meaningful trend, not one isolated seven-day fluctuation.

C. Recomposition

Keep calories near calibrated maintenance where appropriate.

Update weight-dependent macros.

Consider strength progression and available body measurements.

Avoid unnecessary calorie changes.

D. Maintenance

Recalculate maintenance using actual weight trends.

Keep calories close to calibrated maintenance.

Avoid unnecessary adjustments.

E. Universal rule

If the user is progressing at the intended rate:

Recalculate weight-dependent targets.

Update maintenance estimates where justified.

Keep calorie targets unchanged if the existing strategy remains appropriate.

The engine must distinguish between:

Weight-based recalculation.

Maintenance calibration.

Calorie target adjustment.

These are three separate operations.

